    imageAlt: "Military household organizing a Pacific Northwest PCS timeline with moving boxes nearby",
    content: "A PCS move rarely waits for the housing market to cooperate. Orders, report dates, travel, household goods, temporary lodging, financing, and a home sale or purchase may all be moving on different schedules.\n\nThe most useful first step is not choosing a neighborhood. It is building a timeline that separates confirmed information from assumptions and leaves room for changes.\n\n**Start with the dates that control the move**\n\nWrite down every date you know, including the report date, projected departure, available leave, household-goods pickup, temporary-lodging limits, school timing, lease expiration, and any home-sale requirements.\n\nThen mark which dates are firm and which are estimates. A closing date is not guaranteed until the transaction is complete, so avoid making the entire move dependent on one perfect handoff.\n\nUseful backup questions include:\n\n- Where will we stay if closing is delayed?\n- Can our belongings go into temporary storage?\n- Who can attend an inspection if we are traveling?\n- What happens if one household member arrives before the rest?\n- How long can we comfortably carry rent and a mortgage, or two housing payments?\n\n**Choose an area by commute and daily routine**\n\nNaval Station Everett is near downtown Everett, but a reasonable commute can extend into several communities. Everett, Marysville, Lake Stevens, Mukilteo, Snohomish, Arlington, Stanwood, and parts of Bothell offer different combinations of commute time, housing style, services, school assignments, and price.\n\nTest routes during the time you expect to travel. Interstate 5, Highway 9, US 2, and local connections can change considerably by hour and direction. A route that looks short by mileage may not feel short during a shift change or weekday commute.\n\nIf another household member works elsewhere, compare both commutes. The most convenient location for the base may create a difficult daily trip in another direction.\n\n**Decide whether renting first or buying now fits the assignment**\n\nBuying immediately may make sense when the household expects to remain in the area long enough, understands the monthly cost, and can make a careful decision without rushing. Renting first can provide time to learn the area and reduce pressure, but it may involve an additional move and possible lease constraints.\n\nConsider:\n\n- Expected time in the area\n- Cash available after closing\n- Comfort with maintenance and repairs\n- Likelihood of another move\n- Current rental availability\n- Pet and household requirements\n- Whether the purchase would still work if future plans change\n\nNo general rule can decide this for every military household. The choice needs to work at today's payment and under a realistic PCS timeline.\n\n**Using VA financing**\n\nTalk with a VA-experienced lender before touring seriously. The lender determines eligibility, qualification, loan terms, and required documentation. The appraiser evaluates value and minimum property requirements.\n\nAsk the lender about:\n\n- The complete estimated monthly payment\n- Cash needed for closing and reserves\n- Rate-lock timing\n- Appraisal procedures\n- Property-condition concerns\n- Seller-paid costs and lender limitations\n- What could delay final approval\n\nAn agent can help identify visible condition concerns and coordinate the transaction, but cannot guarantee that a property will satisfy appraisal or lending requirements.\n\n**When you cannot attend showings**\n\nA remote showing should be more than a quick video of attractive rooms. Ask to see the street, neighboring properties, exterior condition, storage, mechanical areas, natural light, room scale, traffic or aircraft noise, and anything that differs from the listing photographs.\n\nFor a serious property, use a live walkthrough so you can ask questions in real time. Follow it with a professional inspection and any specialists appropriate to the property. A video tour is useful information, but it is not an inspection.\n\n**PCSing out and selling from a distance**\n\nIf you are leaving before the home is sold, decide who can access the property and approve work. Establish how repairs, cleaning, landscaping, photography, showings, and urgent problems will be handled.\n\nBefore departure:\n\n- Remove documents, medications, valuables, and military items.\n- Arrange insurance coverage appropriate for the home's occupancy.\n- Confirm utility and security plans.\n- Choose local contacts for urgent access.\n- Establish written limits for repair decisions.\n- Plan how offers and closing documents will be reviewed remotely.\n\n**Keep one shared plan**\n\nUse one calendar and one running list for deadlines, contacts, documents, and decisions. Too many separate text chains make it easier to miss something.\n\nA workable PCS plan does not assume that every date will align perfectly. It identifies the housing priorities, financing limits, commute trade-offs, and backup options early enough that a change does not become a crisis."
